<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/plans-clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/plans-clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/plans-clipboard.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>The appraisal path</nav><span class="kicker fade-up">Process guide</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.08s">How Insurance Appraisal Works in Oregon</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.15s">Eight stages from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what, and where Oregon claims most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row fade-up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-w" href="/reach-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts fade-up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Stages</small><b>8</b></div><div><small>Panel</small><b>2 appraisers + umpire</b></div><div><small>Binding</small><b>2 of 3 signatures</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="trail-rail" data-rail aria-hidden="true"><i></i></div>

<h2>Before anyone demands appraisal</h2>
<p>Appraisal is a tool for one kind of disagreement: the insurer agrees the loss is covered, but you disagree on what it costs. Before you invoke it, make sure you have the insurer’s full line-item estimate, your own itemized estimate, dated photos and a copy of your policy, including the declarations page. Run our <a href="/oregon-claim-calculators/appraisal-readiness-checkup/">appraisal readiness checkup</a> to see what’s missing.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Where the clause comes from</b>Most Oregon property policies contain an appraisal clause in their conditions section. Oregon’s insurance code (ORS chapter 742) also sets standard fire-policy provisions that include an appraisal process. Your policy’s wording controls the details, so read it first.</div>
<h2>Stage 1: A disagreement on the number</h2>
<p>Ask for a re-inspection, send a documented supplement and get the insurer’s position in writing. Many disputes close here. If the gap is still meaningful, use the <a href="/oregon-claim-calculators/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">appraisal payoff estimator</a> to see whether appraisal is likely to be worth the cost.</p>
<h2>Stage 2: The written demand</h2>
<p>The demand should be in writing, name the claim number and the date of loss, state that you disagree with the amount of loss, and invoke the appraisal provision. Keep proof of when it was sent and received.</p>
<h2>Stage 3: Each side names an appraiser</h2>
<p>Most clauses require each side to name a “competent and disinterested” appraiser within a set number of days, often 20. Disinterested generally means no financial stake in the outcome beyond a fee, so your contractor is usually a poor choice. Put the deadline on your calendar with the <a href="/oregon-claim-calculators/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a>.</p>
<h2>Stage 4: The appraisers pick an umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers exchange names and agree on a neutral umpire. If they can’t agree within the time the policy allows, either side can usually ask a court to appoint one. Umpire fees are commonly split equally.</p>
<h2>Stage 5: Inspection and estimates</h2>
<p>Your appraiser inspects the property, measures it and writes an independent estimate. The insurer’s appraiser does the same. Good estimates tie every line to a photo, a measurement or a pricing source.</p>
<h2>Stage 6: Working the differences</h2>
<p>The appraisers meet, compare estimates and agree what they can, item by item. This is where most of the gap usually closes. If they agree on everything, they sign an award together and the umpire isn’t needed.</p>
<h2>Stage 7: Umpire review</h2>
<p>Open items go to the umpire, who reviews both appraisers’ support, may inspect the property, and decides each item.</p>
<h2>Stage 8: The signed award</h2>
<p>An award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. A clear award shows replacement cost, actual cash value and depreciation by coverage. The insurer then pays under the policy, applying the deductible, limits and any holdback.</p>
<h2>What appraisal won’t decide</h2>
<ul><li>Whether a loss is covered or excluded</li><li>Claim-handling conduct or unfair claim practices</li><li>Attorney fees or penalties</li></ul>
<p>Those questions belong with the Oregon Division of Financial Regulation, which accepts consumer complaints, or with an Oregon attorney. Oregon’s unfair claim settlement practices statute (ORS 746.230) lists conduct insurers must avoid, and ORS 742.061 can allow a policyholder to recover attorney fees in some lawsuits. Ask an attorney how those rules apply to you.</p>
</article>
